<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liber Paradisus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1256">1256</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liber Paradisus is a law of the commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, issued in 1256 and recorded in 1257, which freed the serfs of the city's countryside by buying their freedom from their lords. It lists the names of nearly 6,000 serfs and their owners, and the freed peasants became taxpayers of the commune. It is often cited as an early act of emancipation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liber-paradisus.jpg" alt="Liber Paradisus" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Plessi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plessi,_Giuseppe_%E2%80%93_Liber_paradisus,_1956_%E2%80%93_BEIC_15137263.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fossalta">Battle of Fossalta</a> in 1249, the expanding Commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> defeated or brought under its authority almost all the lordships (signorie) of the surrounding countryside. The change prompted reflection, ethical and economic, on the status of the serfs, which until then had been fixed by their bond to their lords. Modern historians also see the law as a political and fiscal move: by freeing the serfs, the commune weakened the economic power and military strength of the rural nobility who controlled the farm labour of the Bolognese plains and hills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>On 25 August 1256 the Arengo bell of the Palace of the Podestà called the citizens into the Piazza Maggiore, where the podestà, Bonaccorso da Soresina, and the Capitano del popolo announced the freeing of about 6,000 serfs belonging to some 400 lords. The communal treasury paid the standard market price for each, 8 Bolognese silver lire for a child and 10 for an adult; for 5,855 serfs it paid 54,014 lire in all.</p><p>In 1257 the commune ordered four notaries, among them the jurist Rolandino de' Passaggeri, to record the names and details of the freed serfs in an official register. It is called Paradisus because its first word is Paradise: its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> opening recalls that in the beginning God planted a paradise of delights, placed there the man he had formed, and gave him the most perfect and perpetual freedom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Free men were thought to work the land more productively, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> lost their exemption from the commune's taxes; the commune also forbade them to move outside their diocese. Some were gathered in free places (franco), which gave towns such as Castelfranco Emilia their names. The compensation hastened the move of rural noble families into the city: notarial records show the Lambertini, the Nordigli and the Biancuzzi of Medicina reinvesting in urban property and towers while their estates came under the commune's direct taxation.</p><p>Theological, fiscal and practical concerns together lay behind the mass manumission, and the text itself reads as a treatise on human equality. The register is kept in the State Archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in Piazza dei Celestini. Francesco Saverio Gatta and Giuseppe Plessi published an edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text in 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liber Paradisus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Guelph Bologna captured King Enzo, son of Frederick II, at Fossalta in 1249 and held him until his death in 1272; the <b>Liber Paradisus</b> of 1256 freed the serfs of its countryside.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Republic of Bologna</a></li></ul></section>
